<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lancaster, Pennsylvania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lancaster is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, in a fertile farming county near the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehanna-river">Susquehanna River</a>. The area was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a>, and after their decline the Conestoga lived nearby; in 1763 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paxton-boys">Paxton Boys</a> killed the last Conestoga there. Founded in the 1730s, Lancaster briefly served as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> capital in 1777 and is known for its Amish community.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lancaster-pennsylvania.jpg" alt="Lancaster, Pennsylvania" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Randolph Carney · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lancaster_Pennsylvania_downtown.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lancaster lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, slightly north of the Conestoga River, a left tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susquehanna-river">Susquehanna River</a>. It is the seat of Lancaster County and a hub of Pennsylvania Dutch Country, about 95 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>. The city covers about 19 square kilometres. Its neighbourhoods include Cabbage Hill, named for the cabbage patches kept there by ethnic Germans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settled in the 1720s, Lancaster is one of the oldest inland cities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It was first called Hickory Town and was renamed after the English city of Lancaster by a local man, John Wright; its symbol, the red rose, comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lancaster">House of Lancaster</a>. The site lay within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a>'s charter of 1681 and was laid out by James Hamilton in 1734. It became a borough in 1742 and a city in 1818.</p><p>Lancaster was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> from 1799 to 1812, with the state government at the Court House, built in 1784 and demolished in 1852; the site is now the Soldiers and Sailors Monument at Penn Square. In 1812 the capital moved to Harrisburg. In 2011 the city council recognised 27 September as Capital Day, marking Lancaster's one day as capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1777. Lancaster is also credited with the first Conestoga wagon, used widely for migration before the railroad, and the first Pennsylvania Rifle, made by Martin Meylin in the 1700s. The Hamilton Watch Company released the first battery-powered watch there in 1957. James Buchanan, the 15th president of the United States, is among the city's notable people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>At the 2020 census Lancaster had 58,039 inhabitants. Central Market, built in 1889, is the oldest continuously run farmers' market in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Bethel African Methodist Episcopal Church, built in 1879, aided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> moving north after the Civil War, and its congregation had earlier used a former church as a station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a>. The Fulton Opera House is one of the oldest working <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> in the country, and Franklin and Marshall College is based in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lancaster, Pennsylvania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Susquehannock moved down the Susquehanna River into southern Pennsylvania in the 16th century, settling near modern <b>Lancaster</b> in large palisaded towns.</blockquote><a href="/state/susquehannock">Susquehannock</a></li></ul></section>
